description:
All drawing goes through one small C API (RenderAPI); the desktop implementation uses today's fixed-function GL. PSP (GUM+GE) and Dreamcast (PERFUM+TILE2) implement the same API later — so the API is capped at the intersection of what those can do.

## API (src/render_api.h, no GL includes)
    typedef struct {
        void (*begin_frame)(const FrameCamera* cam, const FrameFog* fog);
        void (*draw_mesh)(const Mesh* m, const float world[16], const float tint[4]);
        void (*draw_ribbon)(const float* pts, int n, float width, const float color[4]);
        void (*billboard)(const float pos[3], float w, float h, const float color[4]);
        void (*begin_2d)(void);
        void (*text_2d)(int x, int y, const char* s);
        void (*rect_2d)(int x, int y, int w, int h, const float color[4]);
        void (*end_frame)(void);
    } RenderAPI;
    extern const RenderAPI render;   // filled by the active backend

Mesh (core type): interleaved {pos, color, texcoord} client-state arrays + counts — no VBOs.

## Compatibility constraints (capped at the GUM/GE + PERFUM/TILE2 intersection)
- Affine texture mapping only (no perspective correction — PS1-era anyway)
- Vertex colours primary; lighting is baked into vertex colours CPU-side at mesh init (single directional light) so every backend looks identical — no runtime fixed-function lighting
- Linear distance fog; alpha-only blending (src_alpha/one)
- One texture per mesh, NEAREST, no mipmaps
- Billboard = camera-facing quad
- No shaders, no VBOs, no colour-ops

## Desktop backend
Port src/render.c -> backend/desktop/render_gl.c implementing RenderAPI via client-state vertex arrays + glDrawArrays. The pass system (island1-m4g4) becomes: core emits FrameScene items in pass order (sky->ocean->terrain->...->HUD); render_gl.c flushes them with its own state diffing (today's gfx.c GfxState diffing lives here).

## Done when
Game renders identically through RenderAPI; zero GL calls outside backend/desktop/; render_api.h has no GL includes; a recording stub backend compiles (smoke test that the API is sufficient).

Deterministic island heightmap: seedable PRNG, coarse grid (PS1-era triangle counts), beach falloff so edges drop below sea level. Expose height_at(x,z) and normal_at(x,z) sampling for gameplay and camera.

## Design
src/terrain.c/h. Grid: 96x96 cells over 192x192 m (~18k tris — PS1-era count).

Deterministic value noise, no RNG state to serialize:

    static uint32_t hash2(int x, int z, uint32_t seed) {
        uint32_t h = seed ^ (uint32_t)x * 0x9E3779B1u ^ (uint32_t)z * 0x85EBCA77u;
        h = (h ^ (h >> 13)) * 0xC2B2AE35u;
        return h ^ (h >> 16);
    }
    // value(x,z) = hash -> [0,1); bilinear between lattice points
    // fbm = v(x,z) * 0.7 + v(x*2, z*2) * 0.3   (2 octaves)

Height:

    float h = (fbm - 0.5f) * 2.0f * AMP;            // AMP ~ 26 m
    float r = length(x, z) / (HALF * 0.85f);        // radial, smoothed corners ok
    h -= smoothstepf(0.75f, 1.15f, r) * DROP;       // DROP ~ 30 m -> rim below 0

Precompute into `float H[96][96]` at init (vertices snap to grid points — the faceted look wants that, not smooth interpolation):

    void  terrain_init(Terrain* t, uint32_t seed);
    float terrain_height_at(const Terrain* t, float x, float z);  // nearest grid sample
    void  terrain_normal_at(const Terrain* t, float x, float z, vec3 out);  // central-difference across the cell, normalized

The mesh task (island1-xf8m) picks grass/sand/rock per vertex from height + slope; props (island1-mvpl) sample the same functions.

## Done when
Same seed -> identical island across runs/platforms; island sits mostly above SEA_LEVEL=0 with a below-water rim forming a beach ring (island1-o116); normals are ~unit length; height_at is fast enough for per-tick collision (island1-md2b).

