REVISED CONTRACT (wipeout-shaped, per user approval) - supersedes the sketch in the description. Still: no miniaudio/KOS types in src/core; headless no-op behavior so GL-free tests keep linking.

Proposed src/core/audio.h surface:
  int  audio_init(void);                    /* device/mixer up; 0=ok; headless stub returns 0 */
  void audio_shutdown(void);
  void audio_reset(void);                   /* scene change: kill reserved loops, stop one-shots */

  /* SFX - fire-and-forget 2D */
  audio_sfx_t *audio_play(int sfx_id);      /* returns handle for post-play tweaks (pitch etc), may be NULL headless */
  /* SFX - 3D positional one-shot: world pos + relative velocity, volume 0..1 */
  audio_sfx_t *audio_play_at(int sfx_id, const float pos[3], const float vel[3], float volume);
  /* SFX - dedicated looping voice (engine hum, ambience): reserved channel, starts silent */
  audio_sfx_t *audio_reserve_loop(int sfx_id);
  /* per-frame update of a live voice: volume 0..1, pan -1..1, pitch scale (1=base rate) */
  void audio_voice_update(audio_sfx_t *v, float volume, float pan, float pitch);
  void audio_voice_set_position(audio_sfx_t *v, const float pos[3], const float vel[3], float volume);

  /* Music - v1: single track, start/replace, loop flag; no playlist modes */
  void audio_music_play(int track_id, int loop);
  void audio_music_stop(void);
  void audio_music_pause(int paused);       /* DC: volume-0 keeps stream running (wipeout trick) */
  void audio_set_music_volume(float v);     /* 0..1 */
  void audio_set_sfx_volume(float v);
  void audio_set_master_volume(float v);

3D math (falloff/pan/doppler) lives game-side in core (portable, testable) and is pushed to backends as vol/pan/pitch, mirroring wipeout sfx.c:277-301. Backends implement a lower-level voice primitive (load raw buffer, play with vol/pan/freq/loop, update params, stop) - on DC that maps 1:1 to snd_sfxmgr + the AICA_CH_CMD_UPDATE packet; on desktop to ma_sound/mixer voices.

IDs: small enum + registry table mapping to asset paths (resolved per-platform like textures). Positional math + node bookkeeping in portable core C => unit-testable without any device (new GL-free test target).

Done when: audio.h + core node/positional logic land, null backend links on all 3 platforms, existing test suite stays green.

description:
Car-like hover physics: position, heading, velocity, throttle/brake, speed-dependent steering, lateral grip that can be exceeded to produce drift, yaw rate from steering * speed, water drag vs land drag. Runs on the fixed timestep.

## Design
src/car.c/h. World units: meters, tick = 1/60s.

    typedef struct {
        vec3  pos, vel;      // world space
        float heading;       // yaw, radians, +Z forward
        float yaw_rate;      // smoothed
        float speed_kmh;     // for HUD
        bool  on_water;
    } CarState;

Tick body:

    // 1. control axes from input: throttle, brake in [0,1]; steer in {-1,0,1}
    vec3 fwd   = { sinf(h), 0, cosf(h) };
    vec3 right = { fwd[2], 0, -fwd[0] };
    float v_f = glm_vec3_dot(vel, fwd), v_r = glm_vec3_dot(vel, right);

    // 2. longitudinal
    v_f += (throttle*ACCEL - brake*BRAKE) * dt;
    v_f -= DRAG*(on_water ? WATER_K : LAND_K) * v_f * dt;   // water drag higher
    v_f = clampf(v_f, -MAX_REV, MAX_FWD);

    // 3. lateral grip — the drift knob
    float grip = minf(1.0f, GRIP * dt);
    v_r -= v_r * grip;
    // reduce grip under hard steering at speed -> slide:
    float drift = minf(1.0f, fabsf(steer) * fabsf(v_f) / 18.0f);
    v_r -= v_r * minf(1.0f, grip * (1.0f - 0.7f * drift));  // keep more lateral when drifting

    // 4. yaw: needs speed to turn, smoothed rate
    float yaw_target = steer * YAW_GAIN * clampf(fabsf(v_f) / 8.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f);
    yaw_rate += (yaw_target - yaw_rate) * minf(1.0f, YAW_SMOOTH * dt);
    heading += yaw_rate * dt;

    // 5. recombine + integrate, then collision (island1-md2b) clamps pos and scrubs v_r on hits
    vel = fwd*v_f + right*v_r;  pos += vel*dt;

Tune constants as #defines in car.h (start: ACCEL=9, BRAKE=14, WATER_K=0.9, LAND_K=0.35, GRIP=6, YAW_GAIN=1.9, YAW_SMOOTH=5, MAX_FWD=28 m/s, MAX_REV=8).

## Done when
0-to-top in ~3.5s on water; holds a straight line with no steer input; hard steer above ~40% speed produces visible slide (v_r non-zero, decaying); reversing works; on_water flag flips at sea-level crossings.

Chase camera replacing the free-fly cam: position = car pos + offset rotated by (heading + lag), critically-damped spring follow, look-at point slightly ahead of the car, speed-based FOV widening and pull-back. Feed from the frame loop.

## Design
src/chase_cam.c/h. Keep the fly cam (camera.c) alive on F for dev flight.

    typedef struct {
        vec3 pos, vel;        // spring state
        float heading_lag;    // eased car heading, radians
    } ChaseCam;

Per render frame (dt = frame dt, not tick):

    // 1. lag the heading — the signature "camera catches up" PS1 feel
    float dh = angle_wrap(car.heading - c->heading_lag);
    c->heading_lag += dh * (1.0f - expf(-6.0f * dt));

    // 2. target point behind the car, growing with speed
    vec3 fwd = { sinf(c->heading_lag), 0, cosf(c->heading_lag) };
    float s01 = clampf(car.speed_kmh / 100.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f);
    float dist   = 7.0f + s01 * 4.0f;     // pull-back
    float height = 3.2f + s01 * 1.2f;
    vec3 target = { car.pos[0] - fwd[0]*dist, car.pos[1] + height, car.pos[2] - fwd[2]*dist };

    // 3. critically-damped spring (stable at dt <= 1/60)
    float w = 8.0f;
    vec3 acc = (target - c->pos) * w * w - 2.0f * w * c->vel;
    c->vel += acc * dt;  c->pos += c->vel * dt;
    // 4. above-terrain clamp (full version: island1-qvn1)
    if (c->pos[1] < terrain_height_at(...)+1.5f) c->pos[1] = terrain_height_at(...)+1.5f;

    look_at = car.pos + fwd * 4.0f + (0, 0.8, 0);
    fov = 58.0f + s01 * 8.0f;   // feed cam_update() / glm_perspective

## Done when
No jitter at constant speed; hard turn shows a visible ~0.3s heading lag then settles (no overshoot beyond a few cm at w=8); at top speed the camera pulls back and FOV widens; terrain clamp keeps the camera above the island on hill crests.

Platform-agnostic input manager: game core sees only named actions + analog axes; each platform backend (desktop GLFW now, PSP/Dreamcast later) fills the same state. No GLFW in core.

Supersedes earlier ACT_BOARD/ACT_EXIT/ACT_TALK mentions in other issues: board/exit = ACT_VEHICLE, pickup/deliver/talk = ACT_INTERACT (one context key — consoles have few buttons).

## Design
src/core/input.h (no platform includes):

    typedef enum {
        ACT_THROTTLE, ACT_BRAKE, ACT_BOOST, ACT_INTERACT, ACT_VEHICLE,
        ACT_LOOK, ACT_PAUSE, ACT_QUIT, ACT_COUNT
    } Action;

    typedef enum { AX_STEER, AX_LOOK_X, AX_LOOK_Y, AXIS_COUNT } AxisId;

    typedef struct {
        uint8_t held[ACT_COUNT];
        uint8_t pressed[ACT_COUNT];   // rising edge this tick
        uint8_t released[ACT_COUNT];  // falling edge this tick
        float   axis[AXIS_COUNT];     // steer [-1,1]; look = radians accumulated this tick
    } InputState;

    void  input_begin_frame(void);      // once per logic tick: clear edges, snapshot axes
    const InputState* input_state(void);
    bool  input_held(Action a);
    bool  input_pressed(Action a);
    float input_axis(AxisId a);

Backends implement (src/backend/<plat>/input.c):
    void backend_input_init(void);
    void backend_input_poll(void);      // fills pending hardware state; core ticks read the snapshot

Bindings are per-platform tables (hw button -> action); analog inputs map to axes with a deadzone (~0.25) for digital use.

### Desktop (GLFW)
W throttle · S brake · A/D steer axis · Shift boost · E interact · F vehicle · RMB-drag look · ESC quit. Removes g_rmb_down / g_last_mouse / handle_input() from src/main.c:29-102; mouse look becomes AX_LOOK_X/Y.

### PSP (P3, later)
Hardware: D-pad (4-way) · flat sliding analog nub (below D-pad) · Triangle/Circle/Cross/Square face · L/R bumpers · Select/Start.
Provisional: Cross ACT_THROTTLE, Circle ACT_BRAKE, L ACT_BOOST, Square ACT_INTERACT, Triangle ACT_VEHICLE; nub X -> AX_STEER, nub Y -> camera pan; D-pad = steer override when nub in deadzone; Start ACT_PAUSE. No mouse: ACT_LOOK unused, chase cam only.

### Dreamcast (P3, later)
Hardware: D-pad (plus-shaped) · concave analog stick · green A / blue B / yellow X / red Y · Start · L/R analog levers.
Provisional: A ACT_THROTTLE, B ACT_BRAKE, L ACT_BOOST (analog fine), Y ACT_INTERACT, X ACT_VEHICLE; stick X -> AX_STEER; D-pad = steer override; Start ACT_PAUSE.

## Done when
Core compiles without GLFW; GLFW backend fills the same InputState; pressing E gives pressed()==true on exactly one tick; A/D hold gives stable ±1 on AX_STEER; each platform's binding table lives in one place.

comments.text:
Closed: fixed-function state is now declared per draw item, and each backend maps it to its own native mechanism. This closes the "state is a flag on the item, not ad-hoc" half of the issue; the GfxState diffing half moved to backend/desktop in b36ab16/0bb1f00 and is covered by island:layering + island:api-smoke.

## What was actually guessing

The Dreamcast backend had no way to be told what a draw *is*, so it inferred it:

```c
/* src/backend/dreamcast/render_dc.c, before */
static pvr_list_t dc_list_of(const RDraw *d) {
  if (d->screen_space && d->pass == R_PASS_HUD)  /* HUD -> PT so it renders last */
    return PVR_LIST_PT_POLY;
  if (d->blend)                                  /* else guess from the blend flag */
    return PVR_LIST_TR_POLY;
  return PVR_LIST_OP_POLY;
}

/* and for screen-space depth: */
float z2d = 1.0f;
if (d->pass != R_PASS_HUD)                       /* backends reaching into the pass enum */
  z2d = (frame->fog_end > 0.0f) ? s_znear / frame->fog_end : 0.001f;
```

Two consequences:
- `screen_space` was doing double duty as "is this HUD?", so the **sky** (screen-space, but scene geometry that must sit behind the island) was indistinguishable from a HUD quad.
- `R_PASS_*` exists for the F3 stats overlay, so having the renderer branch on it coupled two unrelated concerns. Adding a new pass would silently change render order.

## The declaration

`RComposite` in src/core/render.h states the class; `screen_space` keeps its single job (pick the projection), and is now orthogonal:

```c
typedef enum {
    R_COMPOSITE_OPAQUE,      /* sorts with the scene, writes depth          -> PVR OP  */
    R_COMPOSITE_CUTOUT,      /* alpha-tested, per-pixel discard             -> PVR PT  */
    R_COMPOSITE_TRANSLUCENT, /* alpha-blended over the scene, back to front -> PVR TR  */
    R_COMPOSITE_OVERLAY,     /* HUD/2D: over everything, ignores depth      -> PVR PT  */
    R_COMPOSITE_COUNT
} RComposite;   /* + RDraw.composite */
```

OVERLAY shares PT with CUTOUT because the PVR has no overlay list and PT is the one that renders *after* translucent — which is exactly the "over everything" ordering. The core states the intent, the backend picks the bin, so no PVR/GE concept leaks into render.h (island:layering strips comments and still passes).

Each emitter now declares its own class, so there is no inference step left to get wrong: `r_mesh`/`mesh_quad` -> OPAQUE, `r_billboard`/`r_ribbon` -> TRANSLUCENT, `r_rect_2d`/`r_text_2d` -> OVERLAY, sky and ocean -> OPAQUE (the sky is the case that matters: screen-space but scene).

## Backends

DC is now a straight switch with no guessing, and screen-space depth is keyed off the class:

```c
static pvr_list_t dc_list_of(const RDraw *d) {
  switch (d->composite) {
  case R_COMPOSITE_OPAQUE:    return PVR_LIST_OP_POLY;
  case R_COMPOSITE_CUTOUT:
  case R_COMPOSITE_OVERLAY:   return PVR_LIST_PT_POLY;
  case R_COMPOSITE_TRANSLUCENT: return PVR_LIST_TR_POLY;
  default:                    return PVR_LIST_OP_POLY;
  }
}
static float dc_depth_2d(const RenderFrame *frame, const RDraw *d) {
  if (d->composite == R_COMPOSITE_OVERLAY) return 1.0f;   /* nearest */
  return (frame->fog_end > 0.0f) ? s_znear / frame->fog_end : 0.001f;
}
```

List choice and z2d are provably identical to the old logic for every draw the game currently emits (HUD passes were the only `pass == R_PASS_HUD` draws and all are OVERLAY; every `blend` draw is TRANSLUCENT; every other draw is OPAQUE), so this is a behaviour-preserving refactor on DC.

PSP benefits as predicted — the GE has no per-draw polygon lists, so the class reaches hardware through depth:

```c
/* src/backend/psp/render_gu.c, before: overlay-ness inferred from screen_space */
set_dtest(!screen);
set_dwrite(screen ? 0 : d->depth_write);
/* after */
int overlay = (d->composite == R_COMPOSITE_OVERLAY);
set_dtest(!overlay);
set_dwrite(overlay ? 0 : d->depth_write);
```

The sky previously had depth testing disabled purely because it was screen-space; it worked only because it is emitted first and the buffer is cleared. That coincidence is gone — a screen-space scene draw now keeps depth testing.

## Verification

- `meson test -C build`: 7/7. island:api-smoke extended to assert the declarations, including the load-bearing case that screen_space != overlay: every HUD draw must be OVERLAY *and* screen_space, and the sky must be OPAQUE. Observed: OPAQUE=11 CUTOUT=0 TRANSLUCENT=5 OVERLAY=14.
- island:layering passes (src/core still free of GL/PVR/GE symbols).
- Desktop, PSP and Dreamcast all build warning-free; island.prx and island.elf regenerate.
- Desktop output is unchanged by construction: render_gl.c was not touched, it still drives state from `blend`/`screen_space`/`depth_write`, and `composite` is consistent with those on every draw. I did not do a fresh side-by-side screenshot comparison in this session — the earlier pixel-identical check stands from 0bb1f00.

CUTOUT is declared but nothing emits it yet; it exists so island1-qb9x (alpha-tested foliage) has a home and lands on the PT list + GU_ALPHA_TEST without another backend guess.

Unblocks island1-n7to (HUD/minimap), which can now treat HUD panels as OVERLAY draws instead of inventing a new pass.

description:
Replace ad-hoc main.c frame handling with an accumulator loop: 60Hz logic tick, render as fast as vsync allows, clamped dt, frame counter used by physics/animation.

## Design
Fixed tick `TICK = 1.0/60.0`; cap accumulator backlog at 5 ticks to avoid the spiral of death. Expose `uint64_t g_frame_no` and `double g_time` to subsystems.

Replace the while-loop in src/main.c:181-201 with:

    double last = glfwGetTime();
    float acc = 0.0f;
    while (!glfwWindowShouldClose(g_window)) {
        double now = glfwGetTime();
        float frame_dt = (float)(now - last); last = now;
        if (frame_dt > 0.25f) frame_dt = 0.25f;   // clamp hiccups
        acc += frame_dt;
        int ticks = 0;
        while (acc >= TICK && ticks < 5) {
            input_begin_frame();      // snapshot input for this tick
            car_step(&g_car, &input_state, TICK);
            delivery_step(&g_deliv, &g_car, TICK);
            acc -= TICK; ticks++;
        }
        if (ticks == 5) acc = 0.0f;   // drop the backlog
        render(accumulator_alpha = acc / TICK);  // alpha for future interpolation
        glfwSwapBuffers(g_window);
        glfwPollEvents();
    }

## Details
- Step order per tick: input snapshot -> car physics -> delivery logic.
- Mouse-look (RMB drag) stays per-event; the chase camera integrates its spring in render with frame dt (see island1-fnss).
- Render must still run when zero ticks execute (minimized window, high refresh).
- Verification: hold W and read out speed — constant regardless of refresh rate; log tick rate to confirm ~60Hz.

## Done when
main.c loop matches the sketch, `ticks==5` path is exercised (temporarily set TICK=0.05f in a test), and no subsystem reads glfw time directly.
